<meta type=article title="Numbering-registry extension fixture (Phase 3 slice 3a)">
</meta>

<section | Theorem family — shared and own counters>

The theorem family has three counter assignments per the amsthm
convention: `<theorem>`/`<lemma>`/`<corollary>`/`<proposition>` share
one counter; `<definition>` has its own; `<example>` has its own;
`<remark>` and `<proof>` are unnumbered.

This slice's work makes the registry-side numbering live —
cross-references resolve to "Theorem N" etc. The visible label on the
element itself ("Theorem 1.") is deferred to slice 3b (frameable build).

<theorem #thm:pyth | If $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$ then the triangle is right-angled.>

<lemma #lem:zorn | Every nonempty partially ordered set in which every chain has an upper bound contains a maximal element.>

<corollary #cor:odd | Every prime greater than 2 is odd.>

<proposition #prop:cs | For real $a_i, b_i$: $(\sum a_i b_i)^2 \le (\sum a_i^2)(\sum b_i^2)$.>

<definition #def:group | A *group* is a set $G$ with a binary operation satisfying associativity, identity, and inverses.>

<example #ex:integers | The integers $\mathbb{Z}$ under addition form a group.>

<remark | This remark element is unnumbered by design.>

<proof | By similar triangles. ∎>

<section | Cross-references>

Cross-referencing the shared theorem counter: <ref @thm:pyth> is the
first (Theorem 1), <ref @lem:zorn> is the second (Lemma 2),
<ref @cor:odd> is the third (Corollary 3), <ref @prop:cs> is the fourth
(Proposition 4). The shared counter runs across all four propositional
types.

<ref @def:group> and <ref @ex:integers> each start their own counters
(Definition 1, Example 1 — independent of the theorem counter).

<section | Math environments — equation counter>

The five math-environment tags (`<matrix>`, `<cases>`, `<align>`,
`<eqnarray>`) plus the long-form `<math>` now share the `equation`
counter with the existing `<$$>` display-math sigil. Each gets a
visible `(N)` equation-number span after the KaTeX output.

<align #eqn:pyth>
a^2 + b^2 &= c^2 \\
x^2 + y^2 &= r^2
</align>

<eqnarray #eqn:funcs>
f(x) &=& x^2 \\
g(x) &=& 2x
</eqnarray>

The above are equations 1 and 2. Reference to <ref @eqn:pyth> resolves
to "equation 1".
